  "answer": "The FGF signaling pathway plays a significant role in hair-follicle growth and cycling, as evidenced by the findings in the provided literature. While the primary focus of the abstracts is on FGFs in cancer and cartilage development, one abstract directly addresses the role of FGF signaling in hair follicle biology.\n\nIn the context of hair follicle growth, the study highlights the involvement of extracellular vesicles (EVs) derived from human transformed skin-derived precursors (htSKPs). These EVs, particularly those enriched with miR-221-3p, modulate the Wnt signaling pathway, which is crucial for hair follicle development and cycling. The Wnt pathway is known to regulate the anagen (growth) phase of the hair follicle cycle, and its activation by htSKP-EVs promotes the proliferation of hair follicle stem cells (hHFSCs) and dermal papilla cells (hDPCs). This leads to enhanced hair follicle growth and the maintenance of the anagen phase.\n\nThe miR-221-3p within the htSKP-EVs suppresses DKK2, a known inhibitor of the Wnt pathway, thereby activating the Wnt signaling cascade. This activation is essential for the transition of hair follicles into and through the anagen phase, supporting sustained hair growth. The study also validates these findings in both in vitro and in vivo models, including a murine alopecia model, demonstrating the therapeutic potential of FGF-related signaling in regenerative medicine for hair growth.\n\nIn summary, the FGF signaling pathway contributes to hair-follicle growth and cycling by modulating the Wnt pathway through the release of extracellular vesicles containing miR-221-3p, which suppresses DKK2 and promotes the anagen phase of the hair follicle cycle. This mechanism highlights the importance of FGF signaling in maintaining hair follicle function and offers new therapeutic avenues for alopecia treatment.",
